course: dictionary
lesson: 5694
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have several meanings, and lodge is a good example. Read the words around it to find out which meaning the writer means. «In the middle of the pond the family builds a lodge of sticks and mud with an underwater door, so wolves and bears cannot get in.» Ponds, sticks, mud and wolves tell you that a lodge here is a home, the home of a beaver.
explain: Lodge can also be an action word, a verb. Look at «a bone lodged in its throat». The clue is the bone stuck in the throat: lodged means stuck firmly somewhere, so it cannot move. Ask yourself: does this meaning fit the sentence?
explain: Use the swap test: put your meaning in place of the word and check it still makes sense. A beaver's home can be a lodge, and a bone that is stuck firmly is lodged. The context decides which meaning is right.
fact: Lodge can be a noun (a thing), so you can have a lodge or lodges. It can also be a verb (an action), so you can lodge, lodges, lodged or lodging.
fact: A lodge is a small house or cabin, often in the country, the mountains or a park, used for holidays, hunting or skiing.
fact: A beaver's home is also called a lodge. «Beavers build a lodge of sticks and mud» in the middle of a pond.
fact: To lodge means to become stuck firmly somewhere, as in «a bone lodged in its throat».
fact: Lodge can also mean to stay in someone's house and pay rent, or to officially make a complaint, as in «lodge a complaint».
exercise: «In the middle of the pond the family builds a lodge of sticks and mud.» What is a lodge in this sentence? | *A beaver's home made of sticks and mud | A bathing machine on wheels | A railway station | A pond full of fish
exercise: «A bone lodged in its throat.» What does lodged mean here? | Was cooked slowly | Moved quickly away | *Became stuck firmly | Was swallowed happily
exercise: «Dad wants to lodge a complaint about the noisy neighbours.» Lodge is a verb here. What does it mean? | *To officially make a complaint or request | To stay and eat dinner | To sing very loudly | To build a home of sticks
test: «The Smiths will lodge with us for a week and pay rent.» What does lodge mean here? | To lose a game | To get stuck in a throat | To build a dam across a river | *To stay in someone's house and pay rent
test: Which meaning of lodge is used in «a ski lodge»? | A beaver's dam | To make an official complaint | A bone stuck in a throat | *A small house in the mountains, used for holidays or skiing
exam: Why does lodge mean different things in «a ski lodge» and «a bone lodged in its throat»? | The dictionary is wrong about one of them | *One word can have several meanings, and the context shows which one is meant | One of the words is a spelling mistake
